<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Chichen Itza</h1><p class="st-meta">750 CE – 1200 CE (approx.) · capital Chichen Itza</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The dominant city of northern Yucatán in the Terminal Classic and Early Postclassic, whose Castillo pyramid and Great Ballcourt are icons of Maya civilisation.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Chichen Itza grew around two natural sinkholes (cenotes) in north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán</a>. Inscriptions show it was an important centre by the 9th century, when the lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kakupakal">K'ak'upakal</a> was active, and it rose to dominate the north as Classic cities declined.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Chichen Itza seems to have been governed by a council of nobles rather than a single divine king. Its art combined Maya traditions with central Mexican features such as feathered serpents, warrior columns and chacmool figures, once explained as a Toltec conquest but now seen as wider interaction. It controlled coastal trade in salt and other goods. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-cenote">Sacred Cenote</a> received offerings of gold, jade and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Chichen Itza lost its political dominance around 1200, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/league-of-mayapan">League of Mayapan</a> took its place, though pilgrims still visited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-cenote">Sacred Cenote</a> at the Spanish conquest. It became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1988.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/chichen-itza.jpg" alt="Chichen Itza" width="320" height="171"><figcaption>Image: Daniel Schwen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chichen_Itza_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>750 CE – 1200 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chichen Itza</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>K'ak'upakal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Yucatec Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chichén Itzá</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=975&amp;focus=chichen-itza" aria-label="Chichen Itza on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2296 1412 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chichen Itza lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2616 1668L2591 1670"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2591 1670h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2616 1668h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2616 1668h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=975&amp;focus=chichen-itza">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
